8fe36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Related Rates With Rectangular Solids" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064452 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "73d951b2-ddb7-5e7a-805a-d3f9c5c7afd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064724 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064724 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064724 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064724 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f5/f504ea91bae5d27b3e7b31d697ed06c9e34d838724e8dafac46e69d84de9c3cf.png)\n\nA gas station has a rectangular gasoline storage tank stored underground. The tank is $15\\text{ feet}$ long and $10\\text{ feet}$ wide. A worker lowers a dip stick into the tank and notices that the depth of the gasoline is decreasing by $2\\text{ feet}$ every hour. How fast is the volume of gasoline decreasing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064724 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51e84601-668c-5024-8729-dea441ebbfac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064724 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064724 #edb/ref 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the gasoline in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=15\\,\\text{ft},\\,W=10\\,\\text{ft},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{hour}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of the gasoline in the tank:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(15)(10)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =150\\cdot (-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gasoline level is decreasing at a rate of $300\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/hour}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065313 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065313 #edb/ref 1061 897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065313 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33231a76-9fca-526a-a863-28a8b9ba5ab0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065313 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065314 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065314 #edb/ref 1061 898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de101021-b5be-55c7-a7f9-401670121e58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065315 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065315 #edb/ref 1061 901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065315 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eecc2511-6d16-5fc5-877c-1bd58b5fd752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065315 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065316 #edb/ref 1061 904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065316 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fcc1570-0b7e-5714-8da4-85c43cb9a511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065316 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065383 #edb/ref 1064 "We know how to solve related rates problems in the context of squares. We can use the same methods to solve related rates problems in the context of cubes, too.\n\nIn general, to solve a related rates problem, we need to follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram and identify the variables that change with time.\n\n**Step 2:** Write down a relation between the time-dependent variables.\n\n**Step 3:** Differentiate the relation with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nLet's see this in action in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065383 #edb/ref 1065 484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065383 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ff60fc65-18a2-5e55-a0e1-0ecbd0698507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065383 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375544 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375544 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b2/b2158da0465250d14ce47909e7f1ef918c06c9721547a3533e1c65797cc9fab8.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the $5\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. How fast is the surface area of the rectangular prism decreasing when its dimensions are $3\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c144d86d-616b-51ad-b91f-c522e265b17b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375544 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =10\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=5\\,\\text{cm}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=10x+2xy+10y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10x+2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+10)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(10+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 3+10)(5)+(10+2\\cdot 10)(-3) \\\\  & =80-90 \\\\  & =-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186375550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186375548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375545 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "05433573-3744-5b22-9ee4-30694c12c8a1" #edb/ref 175921860454