592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501127 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501127 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501127 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f60c8b13-b572-54f8-b997-4b367f683cdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501129 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8097c0f-208a-5485-aa29-effca04536fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501149 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501149 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501149 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501149 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c11298d151c2f93fbe7d00352386ff859f788ced2641ebe724fd7ddd9f7d205.png)\n\nA cube-shaped inflatable toy is being filled so that its surface area is growing at a rate of $150\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is its edge increasing when each edge is $5\\text{ in}?$\n\nThe cube's edge is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501149 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c009d1d-9f23-53f4-ac53-e3aab6d30ee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501149 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=150\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 150 & =12(5)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are increasing at a rate of $2.5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ea/ea7c818a6694ba959c5230dcde8611a7b1dc44bcad383f54c93d2934336c35b6.png)\n\nThe edges of a cube decrease at a rate of $9\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the volume decreasing when the length of each edge is $40\\text{ cm}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d8efcf9-a827-5e33-8982-8c950b1390fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501156 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=40\\text{ cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=40$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3(40)^{2}\\cdot (-9)\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-43\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}$ at the moment when $x=40\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501157 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7a/7aad5aea708422b331b19ab2e9c7586b5b037054347841530db829e2fc3c9075.png)\n\nIf the surface area of a cube is increasing at a rate of $144{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $4\\text{ cm}?$ Assume that each edge is increasing at the same rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38e9b5c8-f7c2-5d27-9b07-5dff34e427b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501157 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=144\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 144 & =12(4)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are changing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501165 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501165 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501165 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7a/7aad5aea708422b331b19ab2e9c7586b5b037054347841530db829e2fc3c9075.png)\n\nIf the surface area of a cube is increasing at a rate of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $2\\text{ cm}?$ Assume that each edge is increasing at the same rate." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e4521f6-9e6d-5c0e-807a-ed8494a208e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501165 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 48 & =12(2)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are changing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
